id: design.telemetry-pipeline
kind: design
title: Telemetry pipeline
aliases: telemetry, metrics pipeline, high throughput ingest
tags: architecture, observability, scale
questions:
  - how would you ingest 100k metrics per second?
invariant: High-volume telemetry must be aggregated and batched as early as possible, and losing some samples is acceptable by design.
answer_30s: Batch and aggregate at the agent, ship with bounded buffers and sampling, aggregate again at the collector, and accept loss under pressure because telemetry is not a system of record.
answer_2m: |
  The design differs from a transactional pipeline in one important way: loss is
  allowed, so every stage can shed load. That makes the problem tractable:
  agents aggregate (counters, histograms) so 100k samples/s become far fewer
  series/s; collectors batch writes and can drop or sample under pressure;
  storage is dimensional and expects high write throughput with compaction, not
  per-row updates. Cardinality is the real capacity limit, not volume: a label
  with unbounded values multiplies series count and takes down the metrics
  backend, so the agent side must enforce label policy. Numeric maths: 100k
  samples/s at a few bytes each is only a few MB/s, so if the system struggles
  it is almost always cardinality, per-sample overhead or an unbuffered hop, not
  raw bytes.
when_to_use:
  - metrics, traces and logs at fleet scale; anything whose consumer tolerates sampling
avoid_when:
  - business events where loss is unacceptable (that is an event pipeline with durability)
minimal_example: |
  apps (aggregate in-process)
    -> agents on nodes (batch, bounded buffer, drop on overflow)
    -> collectors (batch, cardinality limits, sampling)
    -> time-series store (compaction, retention tiers)
failure_modes:
  - unbounded label cardinality, which is what actually takes down metrics backends
  - no shedding policy, so backpressure from the store blocks the application
  - per-sample network writes instead of batching
related:
  - design.observability
  - design.backpressure
  - design.load-shedding
  - design.bounded-queue
  - recipe.high-throughput-telemetry
sources:
  - https://prometheus.io/docs/practices/naming/
  - https://opentelemetry.io/docs/collector/
